---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/catalogue';
import { scope } from '../../lib/catalogue';
import { unemploymentMethod, unemploymentComparison } from '../../lib/unemployment';
const registered = unemploymentMethod('registered-unemployment-annual')!;
const ilo = unemploymentMethod('ilo-unemployment-annual')!;
---
<Layout title="Methodology">
  <section class="page-heading"><div class="eyebrow">METHODOLOGY</div><h1>Know what<br /><em>you compare.</em></h1><p>The repository separates source evidence, canonical observations, derived measures and presentation.</p></section>
  <div class="prose"><section id="scope"><h2>What this atlas covers</h2><p>{scope.statement}</p><p>Ten modules and roughly 40–50 selected indicator families connect the real economy, households, finance, government and institutions. The annual post-war view centres on 1946–2026; selected long-run, foreign and cantonal comparisons are later extensions.</p><p><a href={href('scope/')}>Read the governing scope, ten modules and remaining-data priorities →</a></p></section><section><h2>Four layers of evidence</h2><ol><li><strong>Sources:</strong> exact dataset references, retrieval dates, usage terms and immutable permitted downloads.</li><li><strong>Canonical observations:</strong> native periods and units, explicit missing values, source references and independent observation and revision statuses.</li><li><strong>Derived measures:</strong> calculations with named inputs and documented conventions.</li><li><strong>Presentation:</strong> independent topic memberships, reusable chart presets, annotation records and presentation colours.</li></ol></section>
  <section><h2>Time and status matter</h2><p>A completed annual observation, a current reading and a full-year forecast answer different questions. The annual chart workspace preserves observation and revision statuses. Reconstructed lines are dotted, forecasts dashed, provisional observations hollow and revised observations square.</p><p>Observed, reconstructed and forecast values are distinguished from final, provisional and revised publication statuses. Missing values remain missing; they never become zeros or interpolated histories.</p></section>
  <section><h2>Preserve statistical identities</h2><p>Registered and ILO unemployment remain separate. Historical monetary instruments and modern policy rates keep their own identities. Historical bond measures do not become ten-year spot rates by changing a label. Federal and general-government debt retain their institutional coverage.</p></section>
  <section id="productivity-hours"><h2>Hourly labour productivity and total hours</h2><p>The two FSO WPS series cover 1991–2025 and use the same 28 August 2026 source vintage. <strong>Hourly labour productivity</strong> is real output per actual hour, published as an index with 1991 = 100. It is not nominal CHF per hour, output per worker, pay or individual performance. <strong>Total actual hours</strong> are annual labour input in millions of hours for the Swiss domestic economy, not hours per person/job or contractual time.</p><p>The productivity CSV publishes compatible GDP and hours indices. We check every year that productivity equals 100 times the real GDP index divided by the hours index. The physical hours totals come from column C of the companion current-price workbook; their 1991-based index matches the denominator in that CSV. We select only actual hours, not the workbook's nominal productivity column. Productivity is not recomputed from an independently sourced atlas GDP vintage.</p><p>The FSO harmonised pre-2010 hours after the 2021 Work Volume Statistics revision to preserve continuity. These WPS hours differ from unadjusted WV history; the atlas does not splice them or add an artificial 2010 gap. For example, the retained WPS 2025 total is about 8.088 billion hours. A separately dated headline WV release should not replace this denominator without reconciliation.</p><p>Source flag R means <strong>revised</strong>, not provisional: 2023–2025 retain that status. The hours observations carry both source snapshots because the companion CSV supplies their revision flags. All years are completed annual observations; no full-year 2026 value or pre-1991 history is invented. An index value of 149.4 represents about 49.4% more real output per hour than in 1991, not CHF 149.4/hour.</p><p><a href="https://www.bfs.admin.ch/asset/en/ts-x-04.07.01.01">FSO real hourly-productivity source ↗</a> · <a href="https://www.bfs.admin.ch/asset/en/je-e-04.07.04.01">FSO compatible total-hours source ↗</a> · <a href="https://dam-api.bfs.admin.ch/hub/api/dam/assets/36731796/appendix">Definitions and revision flags ↗</a> · <a href="https://github.com/duracell04/swiss-economic-atlas/blob/main/docs/source-audit/productivity-hours.md">Exact source audit and reproduction →</a></p><p>Both assets identify OPEN-BY: open reuse with source attribution. Data retain these terms rather than the MIT software licence.</p></section>
  <section id="exchange-rates"><h2>The franc: bilateral and effective exchange rates</h2><p><strong>Lower CHF per foreign currency means a stronger franc.</strong> CHF/EUR covers 1999–2025 using the ECB's published annual averages. CHF/USD covers 1971–2025 using the Federal Reserve's published averages of New York noon buying rates. Historical CHF/DEM covers 1950–1998 using Bundesbank daily Frankfurt quotations: convert each DEM-per-100-CHF fixing x to 100/x CHF per DEM, then average the valid daily fixings within the calendar year. This is an atlas calculation, not the inverse of a published annual mean.</p><p>DEM years require observations in every month and at least 200 valid fixings. Holidays and documented exchange closures are not filled. The incomplete December 1949 start is excluded; the 1953 conversion/agreement-to-official-fixing change remains marked. DEM and EUR are separate series: no pre-1999 euro or post-1998 mark is manufactured. These sources use different market fixing conventions; cross-series ratios do not reproduce simultaneous transaction prices.</p><p><strong>Higher effective indices mean a stronger franc.</strong> The nominal and CPI-based real overall indices are the SNB's published annual means for 1973–2025, with 2000 = 100. The real index adjusts for Swiss and partner-country consumer prices; it is not the PPI-based variant. Competition weights incorporate imports, exports and third-market effects using the IMF approach and a chained Törnqvist index. The partner pool changes from 15 through 1999 to a threshold-based selection from 54 countries from 2000. We retain the published modern-method backcast, not a splice to the discontinued export-weighted index.</p><p>All five charts show complete calendar years only, with no 2026 annual observation. An index of 100 is a reference base, not fair value. SNB's own effective indices carry non-commercial source terms; the atlas does not redistribute its separate Bloomberg-based bilateral quotes.</p><p><a href="https://data.ecb.europa.eu/data/datasets/EXR/EXR.A.CHF.EUR.SP00.A">ECB euro series ↗</a> · <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/releases/g5a/current/">Federal Reserve annual rates ↗</a> · <a href="https://api.statistiken.bundesbank.de/rest/data/BBEX3/D.CHF.DEM.AA.AC.000?format=csv">Bundesbank historical fixings ↗</a> · <a href="https://data.snb.ch/en/topics/ziredev/cube/devwkieffia">SNB annual effective indices ↗</a> · <a href="https://data.snb.ch/en/topics/ziredev/doc/explanations_ziredev">SNB index methodology ↗</a></p><a href="https://github.com/duracell04/swiss-economic-atlas/blob/main/docs/source-audit/exchange-rates.md">Exact source audit and reproduction →</a></section>
  <section id="unemployment"><h2>Registered unemployment and ILO unemployment</h2><p><strong>SECO — registered unemployment (registrierte Arbeitslosigkeit).</strong> {registered.definition} The monthly rates are available through the SNB data portal. The atlas derives its annual rate only from twelve complete, unadjusted monthly rates for the same calendar year. The retained annual series covers 1984–2025; the partial 2026 monthly readings do not produce a full-year observation.</p><p><strong>FSO (BFS) — ILO unemployment (Erwerbslosigkeit gemäss ILO).</strong> {ilo.definition} This is measured by the Swiss Labour Force Survey (SLFS / SAKE). The atlas uses the FSO's published annual means for 2010–2025, rather than calculating them from quarterly rates. The 2021 survey change remains marked.</p><p>{unemploymentComparison} Both rates use a labour-force denominator, with the respective source's population and measurement conventions. They are kept as two independent series with their own historical coverage; neither is used to extend the other.</p><p>Registered jobseekers (registrierte Stellensuchende) are a broader SECO category that also includes people in work or not immediately available. The registered-unemployment series does not represent that entire category. Neither unemployment measure includes everyone outside employment: active search and availability matter for ILO unemployment.</p><p><a href={href('series/registered-unemployment-annual/')}>SECO annual series →</a> · <a href={href('series/ilo-unemployment-annual/')}>ILO / BFS annual series →</a></p><p><a href={registered.reference.url}>BFS glossary: registered unemployed, ILO unemployed and jobseekers ↗</a> · <a href={ilo.reference.url}>BFS ILO definitions ↗</a></p></section>
<section><h2>Overlay indicators</h2><p>Any available annual indicator can share a chart. Matching units share an axis; different units or price bases use separately labelled axes. Values stay in their original units, and each axis scales only its own indicators. Compare the labelled axes when reading magnitudes; overlaid line heights alone do not imply equal values.</p><p>Annual ILO unemployment rates cover 2010–2025 and use the FSO’s published annual means. The 2021 survey change is marked. The verified annual SECO registered-unemployment series covers 1984–2025 and is calculated from twelve complete unadjusted monthly observations; the monthly source series remains available separately for auditability. Annual means, endpoint values and changes keep their original definitions in the indicator information and year readout.</p></section>
<section><h2>Calculations with clear units</h2><p>Growth is a percentage change in a compatible level series. Rebased indices use a fixed documented base period. Subtracting two yields stored in percent produces a difference in percentage points.</p><p>The comparison workspace is annual. Native-frequency source series are retained only when they are themselves verified and useful for audit or for transparent annual derivation. Annual means use complete calendar years; incomplete current years are left out rather than presented as full-year observations.</p></section>
  <section id="publication"><h2>Publication standards</h2><p>A source audit must establish the exact dataset, statistical definition, coverage, revision practice and redistribution evidence. Only verified, permitted real observations enter public downloads.</p><p>Software uses the MIT licence. Each dataset retains its own usage terms, attribution and conditions. The software licence does not grant rights to third-party observations.</p><p>Updates are reviewed before publication. Validation checks provenance, snapshot integrity, periods, duplicate observations and reproducibility. The website is built from committed inputs and makes no live requests to economic data services.</p></section>
  <section><h2>Government yields</h2><p>The long yield uses the SNB historical yield-by-maturity proxy for 1946–1954 and the OECD ten-year government benchmark from 1955. The proxy years are dotted and the 1955 definition change is marked. GMBF begins in 1980; its annual average uses month-end observations, with the last issue or auction in each month.</p><p>The 2026 points are provisional partial-year averages: January–August for the long yield and January–September for GMBF. They are neither completed annual averages nor current yields. The 10Y − 3M spread uses matched completed years through 2025 and has no 2026 observation.</p></section>
  <section><h2>General government gross debt / GDP</h2><p>The broad gross-debt ratio covers 1946–2026. It is distinct from the narrower Maastricht ratio and from Confederation-only debt. The historical component aggregates debt of the Confederation, cantons and municipalities and divides it by historical nominal GDP. It is dotted through 1987.</p><p>The 1988 change to IMF data and the 1990 change to the April 2026 WEO vintage are marked as breaks. Historical and IMF coverage, liability and consolidation conventions differ; these points do not establish a seamless constant-definition history. No Maastricht data are spliced into this line.</p><p>The 2025 reading is a provisional completed-year value. The 2026 reading, 38.50% of GDP, is an IMF forecast shown with a dashed connector. No earlier total-government history is inferred from JST's pre-1946 central-government series. Historical data and adaptations use CC BY-NC-SA 4.0; IMF and ecolod attributions and terms remain attached to the modern observations.</p></section>
  <section id="wages-income">
    <h2>Real wages and median disposable household income</h2>
    <p>The <strong>FSO real gross wage index</strong> covers 1942–2025 with base 1939 = 100. It measures CPI-adjusted wage-price evolution under a fixed employee structure, not median salary, productivity or take-home pay. We select total employees and the real index from the original FSO file. It is already inflation-adjusted; it is not deflated again with the atlas CPI.</p>
    <p>Until 1993 the wage source is the October wage survey and the CPI timing is October. In 1994 the wage data switch to SSUV accident reports and the CPI convention to annual-average variation. A visible break preserves that distinction. The source marks 1942 as an estimate, which the atlas identifies as reconstructed/source-estimated rather than a forecast. No base-year observation or 1940–1941 values are invented. Since 2021 the publisher uses a different precision convention. Growth should come from its growth field, not rounded index levels; the 1942 growth field is a 1939–1942 average, not a single-year rate.</p>
    <p>The <strong>real median disposable household-income chart</strong> uses the FSO Household Budget Survey (HBS/HABE), total population, 2000–2023. Its unit is equivalised CHF per month at 2023 prices. There is one estimate per reference year: monthly amounts do not make this a monthly-frequency series. The FSO has already adjusted these medians using the Swiss CPI. We import its published constant-price series unchanged, not nominal workbook values, a mean, a working-household-only estimate, PPP income or the separate SILC series.</p>
    <p>Household resources include transfers. Disposable income deducts social contributions, taxes, basic health-insurance premiums and regular transfers to other households, but remains before ordinary consumption and rent. Household size is adjusted using the modified OECD scale: 1.0 for the oldest person, 0.5 for each additional person aged 14 or over, 0.3 for younger children. The median is measured across people using their equivalised household income. Imputed rent is excluded.</p>
    <p>The HBS revised weighting model applies from 2015 and cannot be applied to earlier years. The chart disconnects 2014 from 2015; that difference is not a comparable income change. Source 95% confidence bounds are preserved in the original data download, while this atlas view plots point estimates only. Small movements are not automatically statistically significant. HBS reference years are retained without a SILC-style one-year shift. No 2024–2026 income estimate is manufactured.</p>
    <p><a href="https://www.bfs.admin.ch/asset/en/ts-x-03.04.03.02.01">FSO wage source ↗</a> · <a href="https://dam-api.bfs.admin.ch/hub/api/dam/assets/36506632/appendix">Wage definitions and flags ↗</a> · <a href="https://www.bfs.admin.ch/asset/en/36355431">FSO real median-income source ↗</a> · <a href="https://datawrapper.dwcdn.net/4bd288526038674e71356216f4ffb48b/4/dataset.csv">Original income data with 95% intervals ↓</a> · <a href="https://dam-api.bfs.admin.ch/hub/api/dam/assets/36337470/master">FSO income methodology ↗</a> · <a href="https://github.com/duracell04/swiss-economic-atlas/blob/main/docs/source-audit/wages-income.md">Exact source audit and reproduction →</a></p>
    <p>Both dataset assets identify OPEN-BY, permitting reuse with attribution. Attribute the FSO and retain the statistical definitions and price bases. Software licensing does not change the data terms.</p>
  </section>
  <section><h2>What comes next</h2><p>The exchange-rate, productivity/hours and real wage/median-income priorities are now published. Next come housing and credit, trade and current-account flows, and fiscal revenue, expenditure and balance. The <a href={href('scope/#priorities')}>scope roadmap</a> separates these acquisitions from later financial detail and unresolved source permissions. Candidate definitions are a research agenda, not statistical findings.</p></section></div>
</Layout>
